Dear lavaan user,

I hope you are doing well. I'm very new to SEM, currently learning a simulation using simsem package. I have the following population model:

# Latent variables (normal data)
C1 ~= x1 + x2 + x3
C2 ~= x4 + x5 + x6

# Path model (alternative y = C1 + C2 + Z), y is normal
y = C1 + C2 + z1 + z2

# Mediator model (alternative Z = C1 + C2), Z is categorical (1,2,3), z1 and z2 are dummies
z1 = C1 + C2
z2 = C1 + C2

My question is can I create dummy variables z1 (when Z=1) and z2 (when Z=2) derived from the variable Z, which has original values of 1, 2, and 3?
My aim to compare the estimation result from a dummy (z1,z2) vs categorical mediator (Z) variable. Is it possible to do this kind of simulation? Apologize for a very basic question.

I use the following code but not exactly what I want:

library(simsem)
# Population model
popModel <- "
# Factor loadings
C1 =~ 1*x1 + 0.7*x2 + 0.7*x3
C2 =~ 1*x4 + 0.7*x5 + 0.7*x6

# Path model
y ~ 0.5*C1 + 0.5*C2 + 0.2*z1 + 0.2*z2
z1 ~ 0.4*C1 + 0.4*C2
z2 ~ 0.4*C1 + 0.4*C2
"
# Discrete data
variables <- c("z1","z2")
pop.th <http://pop.th> <- paste0(variables, ' | 0*t1')
pop.mod.ord <- c(popModel, pop.th <http://pop.th>)
(dat.ord <- generate(model = pop.mod.ord, n = 5000,parameterization = "theta"))

# Test using Lavaan
model <- '
C1 =~ x1 + x2 + x3
C2 =~ x4 + x5 + x6

y ~ C1 + C2 + z1 + z2
z1 ~ C1 + C2
z2 ~ C1 + C2
'
# Fit data
fit <- sem(model, data = dat.ord)
summary(fit, fit.measures=TRUE, standardized=TRUE)

